1
00:00:47,360 --> 00:01:00,080
People are worried about whether there's going to be an enduring relationship with China and Russia. If you look at this picture, the relations look more glacial than cordial and the little

2
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:13,520
one's hauling on the arm of the big one. One wonders about that. It turns out my expertise is on Russo-Chinese relations. That's what I studied in graduate school.

3
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:23,680
My dissertation was a history of their border from the Opium Wars in the mid-19th century until Outer Mongolia was snatched from the Chinese sphere of influence

4
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:35,520
and parked in the Russian sphere in the 1920s. So it's fun to talk about this particular topic. Before I get going, I'm going to do some terminology.

5
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:48,320
I'm going to use the word Russia to refer to the czarist, Soviet, and modern periods, the same way that you use France to describe its many monarchies and many republics.

6
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:58,320
The Bolsheviks thought they were special, so they came up with special words for special people: Soviet, Soviet Union. But it turns out they were temporary

7
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:12,000
and Russia is the enduring thing. So that's it on terminology. Before I speculate on what the future is going to look like, our only database that we have is whatever happened from this

8
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:23,280
second backwards, what people call history. It's just whatever is in the past. That's it, that's our database. So I'm going to look at when Russia was strong and China was weak,

9
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:34,560
from the mid-19th century to the mid-20th century, then the reversal of the power balance, and then in the recent period when China has been strong and Russia weak.

10
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:42,960
China and Russia discovered each other late in their histories. It was the early part of their last dynasties when the Russians were after

11
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:54,000
fur, very lucrative in those days. That brings them out to the Pacific. But their relations are only episodic until we get to the mid-19th century, which is where my

12
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:08,720
story's going to begin. So in the 18th century, China was strong, Russia was weak. But that doesn't last very long. Both empires followed the rules for continental empire.

13
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:17,600
If you want to survive in a continental world— that's what both of them historically have been—you don't want to have two-front wars because you have multiple neighbors.

14
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:27,840
Any one of them can come in at any time. If they gang up on you, that's trouble. So you take on one at a time. Also you don't want any great powers on the borders. This is the fundamental

15
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:36,880
problem with their relationship. Today's friend can be tomorrow's foe. That is truly problematic. So what do you do to solve that problem?

16
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:45,600
Well, you take on your neighbors sequentially. You set them up to fail. You destabilize the rising, ingest the failing, and you set up buffer zones in between.

17
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:57,520
You wait for the opportune moment to pounce and absorb. That is Vladimir Putin's game. But if you play this game, you're surrounding yourself with failing states, because you're

18
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:10,320
either busy destabilizing them or ingesting them. So the curious might ask, are Russia and China unlucky with all the very dysfunctional places that surround them, or are they complicit?

19
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:19,040
Also, there are no enduring alliances in this world because the neighbors figure it out that the hegemonic power offers nothing but trouble in the long term.

20
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:27,040
There's also no counsel on when to stop expanding. So both Russia and China are known for overextending, overdoing it.

21
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:39,040
That may help explain some of their periodic implosions over their long and bloody histories. Very high mortality rates. Before you dismiss this paradigm, you've seen it operating in

22
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:53,440
real time in Syria and Ukraine. There are people who do this. It also explains why all those ancient ruins are ruins. This sort of warfare is ruinous. But anyway, it lies at the basis of many

23
00:04:53,440 --> 00:05:06,080
of the great civilizations of Eurasia. This is how they did things. I'm going to start my story in the mid-19th century, when the Chinese were beset by a whole

24
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:18,400
series of rebellions that just about wrecked them. The Russians take advantage of all of this. Remember the second rule of continental empires? No great power neighbors. The

25
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:30,160
Russians repeatedly derail the rise of China by scripting the Chinese to do things that are remarkably detrimental to Chinese interests, but pretty good for Russian interests.

26
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:37,520
It takes the Chinese a long time to figure it out. They have governments coming and going in this period. It's a difficult period,

27
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:46,560
but they eventually figure it out by the time Mao reunifies China in 1949. So I'm going to go through each of those examples in turn, starting with a really

28
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:56,560
big one, which is the Opium Wars. This is when Britain and France are coming at China in order to force China to trade on their terms.

29
00:05:56,560 --> 00:06:07,120
This corresponds with the two biggest rebellions of China's period of rebellions, the Taiping and the Nian Rebellion. Here's a big chart. That's a simplified chart. The rebellions

30
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:16,960
start in the late 18th century. Rebellion is a misnomer. These are civil wars. Either people are minority peoples who want out of the empire, they want to secede, or other people who want to

31
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:29,520
overthrow the government in Beijing. The peak period is in red. The really big ones are in white. So China has got the two-front war problem. It's got Europeans coming at them, plus all of this.

32
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:39,360
In fact, the Chinese have so many fronts, they don't know how to deal with it. The Russians come to the Chinese and say, "Hey, we can deal with the

33
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:50,720
British and French for you and solve that problem. You can deal with all the internal stuff. However, we need to have you sign a couple pieces of paper for us, the Treaty of Aigun of 1858 and

34
00:06:50,720 --> 00:07:03,680
the Treaty of Peking of 1860." What do they do? They cede to Russia large swaths of territory in Central Asia and the Pacific coastline. The Qing Dynasty was vague on geography.

35
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:11,760
They're beset by these other things. They don't understand that Europeans think these pieces of paper are permanent things. They figure that once they put their house back

36
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:23,920
in order, they're going to come back and get the territory later. Okay, the second example of ruinous Russian mediation that is going to keep China in turmoil.

37
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:41,440
So in the First Sino-Japanese War, Japan trounces China, boots them from their tributary in Korea. Then the Japanese also want some territory on the Liaodong Peninsula.

38
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:55,280
The Chinese go to the Russians to help them counterbalance Japan so that Japan doesn't take this Chinese territory in the Asian mainland. Russia gets its buddies, France and Germany,

39
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:03,680
the so-called Triple Intervention, to gang up on Japan. Japan looks at three great powers and says, "I don't think so." So they bail. From the

40
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:13,280
Chinese point of view, so far so good. Except what the Russians promptly do is take for themselves the very territory that had just been denied to Japan. The

41
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:25,840
story gets worse. All the European powers, or many of them plus Japan, come in and they carve out big concession areas throughout China. So China's not going to have full sovereignty

42
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:38,960
over its territory for several generations. So instead of one relatively small Japanese concession area, they get foreigners everywhere. Think about the second rule of continental

43
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:47,920
empire, no great power neighbors. That’s not happening while this is going on. The Bolsheviks come to power and then they're going to apply these rules as well.

44
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:58,160
When they do come to power, they're very weak because Russia's been devastated by World War I. The Bolsheviks don't win their own bitter Civil War until 1922.

45
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:09,200
So then as now, they relied on a really cheap but incredibly effective strategy of strategic communication. The Russians really understand

46
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:22,160
other people's emotional life and what sets them at odds with each other. They know just how to serve out the propaganda that sets people at each other's throats.

47
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:35,360
Their propaganda is going to help the Chinese really despise the Japanese and the Europeans, while Russia's even greater predations, the ones you've already seen, go unnoticed. Here's Lev

48
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:47,360
Karakhan. He was a deputy foreign minister. In 1919, he sends a missive, his Karakhan Manifesto, to the Chinese Foreign Ministry. He says, "Hey, we're not imperialists,

49
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:58,080
we're Bolsheviks. We're gonna return all the lands from those unequal treaties and be your friend forevermore, unlike all the other evil

50
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:09,120
imperialist powers. We're not like that anymore." The Chinese are looking at this and thinking, "Wow, here are the Bolsheviks who've gotten rid of their imperialistic government.

51
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:17,120
They're putting together their shattered land." So this offers hope to the Chinese that they can do likewise. It's a model potentially to

52
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:27,440
follow and a mentor who might help them. Except here's the detail. When the Bolsheviks started doing better in their civil war, they really dialed back what their offer was.

53
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:40,000
The original offer was, "tear up treaties, China gets all territory back, no payments necessary." Under the new version of the Karakhan Manifesto, which the Russian Foreign Ministry goes and

54
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:48,880
telegraphs to the Chinese Foreign Ministry. I've seen the document, or at least a copy of it in the archives in Taiwan. They send it back and say,

55
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:56,640
"We're willing to talk about these things. We're going to hold some negotiations." The facts are they didn't return these concessionaries until the mid-20th century,

56
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:08,480
1950s, after the Westerners had returned almost all of their concession areas. This is not trivial. When we think of concession areas in the age of imperialism in China,

57
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:18,160
you think of British ones: Hong Kong. Well, Hong Kong isn't actually very big. The reason you know about Hong Kong is it makes lots of money, or at least it used

58
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:28,960
to. The Russian concessionaries were huge. They didn't make money but what else is new? But the Russians had by far the largest concession area of any other country.

59
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:38,000
But from the Karakhan Manifesto is the origin of the myth of Sino-Soviet friendship, that the Russians somehow treated the Chinese nicely. And the Chinese Foreign Ministry officials,

60
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:46,160
who would've known better, their government is overthrown within the decade. And presumably these documents just gather dust in the archives.

61
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:58,720
My fourth example of derailing China's rise concerns the First United Front between the Chinese Nationalists and Chinese Communist Party. Here's the leader of the Nationalist Party

62
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:09,040
and also leader of its armies, Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek. He led the Northern Expedition, reunifying China at least nominally by either defeating

63
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:19,600
or co-opting all those warlords. There you can see different colors of where the major warlords were. Previous South China attempts to do this or to

64
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:31,520
secede from China, one or the other, had failed for lack of a proficient military. But Russia changes that. It provides aid, arms, and expertise and structures and things to fund the Whampoa

65
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:44,480
Military Academy, which is in Canton/Guangzhou. That institution is going to educate the officers, both Communist and Nationalists, who would lead not only this,

66
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:54,320
but some of their civil war-era officers that make this reunification of China possible. But the Russians had a price. Give the Nationalists the aid,

67
00:12:54,320 --> 00:13:05,440
but the Nationalists then have to let the Communists into the Nationalist Party. That's what the United Front is. This all coincides with a bitter

68
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:14,720
succession struggle in Russia. This is the problem with dictatorships. They really don't do succession well. It's why elections are so convenient.

69
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:26,240
Instead you have Stalin and Trotsky going at it over which one was going to be the big cheese. Stalin is all about socialism in one country. He thinks that Russia ought to focus

70
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:37,840
on its own internal development. Whereas Trotsky says, "Nonsense, we need to focus on world revolution because only if there are sister revolutions abroad can ours survive."

71
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:51,520
While this is all going on, the Chinese Communists really want to get out of that United Front. Why? Because it puts them in close proximity to the army, which is controlled by the Nationalists.

72
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:59,120
They're getting worried whether they're about to get killed. The Russians say "No, no, no, no, it's good. You stay in that United Front." So they

73
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:11,760
do. Chiang Kai-shek goes roaring up China. I've shown you the map. He reaches his home base in Shanghai, pauses, and he turns his guns on the Communists and just massacres them in droves.

74
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:22,640
This is when Mao has to think of a rural strategy to power because the urban strategy is no longer feasible. Once this happens, Stalin can use it to just

75
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:36,160
trounce Trotsky in the internal power struggle because he can say, "Look, see, revolution in one country. It doesn't work abroad." A lot of Chinese died proving Stalin's point.

76
00:14:36,160 --> 00:14:44,800
Another example where Russia literally derails the rise of China. There's a railway system in Manchuria. We're going to talk rails.

77
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:58,400
The Russo-Japanese War of 1904 and 1905, Japan wins it and gets from Russia, which built all these things in Chinese territory, the southern half of that railway net in lieu of an indemnity.

78
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:09,680
Japan invests massively in railways, infrastructure, and apparently local politicians. But the ruling warlord apparently wasn't sufficiently attentive to Japanese needs,

79
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:20,480
so they assassinated him in 1928. His son, Zhang Xueliang, the following year in 1929 decides he wants his railways back from Russia. What does Russia do? It's

80
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:32,960
not either version of the Karakhan Manifesto. The Russians deploy over 100,000 troops, tanks, airplanes, the works, and just pound this man. The Russians keep their railways. So

81
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:41,920
if you want to delay the rise of China, that sort of thing delays the rise of China. But now for the first rule of continental empire: no two-front wars.

82
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:53,280
You move fast-forward to the 1930s and Stalin thinks he may very well face a two-front war with Germans in the west and Japanese in the east. Why would he think such thoughts? The

83
00:15:53,280 --> 00:16:02,880
Anti-Comintern Pact. Comintern is short for Communist International. It is the Soviet outreach program. It's signed in 1936 between

84
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:13,040
the Japanese and the Germans. Stalin goes, "Uh-oh, they're after me." He plays every one of his China cards and he holds lots of them.

85
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:24,320
If you want to disintegrate the neighbors in order to delay their rise, well then you fund all sides of their civil wars and any side in between because you just want them to go at it.

86
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:34,800
So he plays every card he's got. What he wants are the Nationalists to stop fighting the Communists, vice versa, and unite to fight the Japanese.

87
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:44,080
They're willing to do this provided Stalin provides conventional aid, which he does, but they think he's also going to provide soldiers. They don't get it. Once they're in,

88
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:54,400
Russia is out of this thing. Stalin's plan, his script for the Chinese and Japanese, works beautifully. Because when the Nationalists unite in the Second

89
00:16:54,400 --> 00:17:04,560
United Front with the Communists, going back to the dark side, the Japanese are apoplectic. This is when they do the massive escalation in 1937 and they are

90
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:17,440
off to overextension into parts due south of Russia. So this two-front Japanese-German war never materialized. Stalin was very successful, the Chinese less so because the Chinese are fighting

91
00:17:17,440 --> 00:17:28,960
the Japanese so the Russians don't have to. That comes at the price of millions of deaths, millions of refugees, that does indeed derail China's rise yet again. Next example. Per the

92
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:42,160
Yalta agreement, Russia finally gets into the war in Asia. About time. In the very final weeks, in this August Storm the Russians deploy 1.5 million soldiers.

93
00:17:42,160 --> 00:17:53,440
It's one of the largest military operations of World War II. They rapidly take Manchuria. That would be the normal thing, but here's the abnormal thing.

94
00:17:53,440 --> 00:18:06,160
They also take away Manchuria's industrial base. That would not normally be what you do to someone. They take 83% of the electrical power equipment, take it home to Russia, not turning lights on in

95
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:21,280
Manchuria. 86% of mining, 82% of cement making, 80% of metalworking equipment. Plus they take 640,000 Japanese POWs to be slave labor for decades, if they ever get home at all.

96
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:31,680
And they also take the northern islands, which are still under dispute today. But if you think about it, if you're going to do indemnities or reparations or whatever this is,

97
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:41,440
China had been fighting Japan in one form or another for 15 long years. Russia comes in for the cameo performance at the very end.

98
00:18:41,440 --> 00:18:55,520
So if there are indemnities to be paid for whatever Japan did in this war, surely China, not Russia, should have been the recipient for all this stuff. In addition, there’s another example.

99
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:05,600
Not only does Stalin walk away with the industrial base, but he walks away with Mongolia as well. How does that work? The Yalta agreement also stipulates that the status quo

100
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:12,240
shall be maintained in Mongolia. So then you have to look at, well, what was the status quo? It was the Russian sphere of influence in the

101
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:24,720
north, Chinese continuing control in the south. Mongolia, which had always been both those places, had been part of the Qing Empire, never been part of the Russian Empire.

102
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:35,440
Moreover, Stalin had already taken Tannu Tuva in 1944. It looks small on this map, but it's bigger than England. It had lots of gold, which the Soviets had monetized long ago.

103
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:50,000
So if you add up all the territory that the Russians took from the Chinese sphere of influence from the 1858 Treaty of Aigun and 1860 Treaty of Peking, fast-forward to detaching Outer

104
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:59,120
Mongolia from the Chinese sphere of influence. Here's what it really is. It's greater than all US territory east of the Mississippi. This is not your normal land grab.

105
00:19:59,120 --> 00:20:08,880
So talk about derailing somebody, that would do it. To be fair to the Russians, they did, albeit slowly, turn over all this Japanese-stockpiled

106
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:18,480
military equipment in Manchuria to the Communists. They also, albeit belatedly, trained them on how to use the equipment and also how to run the Manchurian railway system.

107
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:29,040
The Chinese Communists, as a rural peasant movement, how would they know how to do these things? They wouldn't. It allows this conventional aid and

108
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:39,520
logistics of being able to move people around. It transforms the Communists from a lightly armed guerrilla movement to a very heavily armed conventional force capable for the

109
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:51,280
showdown phase of the Chinese Civil War. So like the Whampoa Military Academy, this is essential aid for the Communist victory. So neither the Communists or the

110
00:20:51,280 --> 00:21:02,800
Nationalists complain about the deindustrialization of Manchuria. The Communists probably traded that industry for all the conventional aid that they got.

111
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:12,160
The Nationalists traded it and also Outer Mongolian independence for a promise from Stalin not to aid the Communists, a promise that he promptly breaks.

112
00:21:12,160 --> 00:21:20,720
So Mao starts to figure out that something is up here. So when he's on a roll in his offensives in the civil war, there's really bitter fighting.

113
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:31,040
The real movement in the last phase, the post-1940 phase of the Chinese Civil War, is in 1948. That year Mao just moves and he is roaring down south.

114
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:41,200
He's about to get to the Yangtze River and Stalin's like, "Hey buddy, take a break at the Yangtze, don't exhaust yourself." Mao ignores it. Whereas Stalin

115
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:51,920
might have wanted to keep a Nationalist rump state south of the Yangtze River, yielding a divided China in keeping with weakening your neighbor,

116
00:21:51,920 --> 00:22:01,440
Mao is not remotely interested in that. Here's my 10th example, which is the Korean War. If you look at the Korean War, the first year is a war of movement.

117
00:22:01,440 --> 00:22:10,400
It’s up and down, up and down the peninsula. It's unbelievable how much movement there is. But then it stalemates for the last two years and you think, "Well, what's going on?

118
00:22:10,400 --> 00:22:25,120
Why don't they settle the war sooner?" Both sides are taking incredible losses. Well, here's how it goes. Once China intervenes in the Korean War and once they halt various offensives

119
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:36,160
to start peace talks, the Chinese do incredible tunnel work, probably the North Koreans as well, and build an incredible tunnel system. It means the South Koreans and the UN

120
00:22:36,160 --> 00:22:43,280
forces are never going to get anywhere near the Soviet border ever again. From that moment on, Stalin thinks he's got a low-risk,

121
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:53,840
high-reward strategy where he's going to weaken the United States and delay the rise of China. So what's not to love about fighting to the last Chinese in Korea?

122
00:22:53,840 --> 00:23:04,560
Stalin thinks this is great and it's going to retard Chinese development. Also because China is so isolated by this war, it has no international friends but Russia,

123
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:17,520
it's going to tie China to Russia ever more firmly and give Russia breathing space to rebuild after World War II while its Western enemies are wasting time in Korea.

124
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:30,480
If you put it all together—Chinese Civil War, Korean War, Russia's on-and-off again aid to different sides in the civil war, his double-dealing with both of them and

125
00:23:30,480 --> 00:23:39,280
what happened with Outer Mongolia and the Manchurian industrial base, Stalin's advice to Mao to halt at the Yangtze, and then he's fighting to the last Chinese in

126
00:23:39,280 --> 00:23:55,360
Korea—this is all consistent with the second rule of continental empire: no great power neighbors. Once Stalin dies, finest day of his life, there's never as strong a leader in Russia again.

127
00:23:55,360 --> 00:24:04,560
By this time, Mao has figured out that the Russians don't want a strong China. He has to bide his time for a while, but he understands what is going on.

128
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:15,920
Mao has a growing list of gripes. It's not only that he didn't like Stalin's tributary treatment, but also Mao thinks, with his resume, that he should become

129
00:24:15,920 --> 00:24:26,480
the leader of international communism. Stalin's successor, Nikita Khrushchev, is like, "No way." Khrushchev does not remotely have Mao's resume.

130
00:24:26,480 --> 00:24:37,520
Mao has just put together a continent by reunifying China. That's not remotely what Khrushchev's ever been able to do. Mao also can't stand either Khrushchev's domestic or foreign policy.

131
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:47,280
Domestically, Khrushchev is all about de-Stalinization. Mao doesn't like that, he's got a cult of personality. He doesn't want to do things like that. Khrushchev is also interested in peaceful

132
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:56,400
coexistence with the West, or at least nominally, whereas Mao is in the midst of the Cultural Revolution, which is based on a virulently anti-Western foreign policy.

133
00:24:56,400 --> 00:25:08,720
They're also forever squabbling about who's aiding North Vietnam in the Vietnam War and who's going to get credit for it. So all that's going on. Now, Khrushchev has his own gripes about the Chinese.

134
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:17,680
He looks around at the United States. The United States has got basing all over the world. Its allies allow the United States to have bases.

135
00:25:17,680 --> 00:25:28,400
China has hardly any Russian bases, these leftover concession areas, and China wants them back. Khrushchev can't understand this. What he really can't understand are the two

136
00:25:28,400 --> 00:25:42,080
Taiwan Strait crises of 1954 and 1958, where Mao started lobbing ordnance on Nationalist islands that are very close to mainland shores. Khrushchev is apoplectic because Mao

137
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:53,840
hasn't given him any advance warning. By the way, this sort of thing could trigger some of the security clauses of the Sino-Soviet Friendship Treaty with nuclear follow-on effects.

138
00:25:53,840 --> 00:26:04,160
Then while this is going on, Khrushchev wants to have a combined sub fleet base. If we're going to take all these risks, we need to have subs in different places. Mao says,

139
00:26:04,160 --> 00:26:11,760
"No way." Then Khrushchev has had enough and says, "Well, you're not going to get the plans for the atomic bomb." The whole thing becomes public in

140
