00:53:10,400 --> 00:53:20,480
They're going to focus on the evil Japanese, evil Americans, and focus things that way. Well, the problem with nationalism is it's a very heady drink.

282
00:53:20,480 --> 00:53:34,640
If you imbibe too much, it clouds the judgment. Moreover, it repels minority people within China's empire who are not interested in Han nationalism. It frightens neighbors to coordinate with each

283
00:53:34,640 --> 00:53:47,600
other and find big powers to counterbalance this mess. It also impedes the de-escalation of unforeseen international dust-ups or crises.

284
00:53:47,600 --> 00:53:58,400
With Putin's latest adventures, one would assume that the Chinese have some real thoughts about what's going on now. In Putin's case, he has thrown away the Soviet

285
00:53:58,400 --> 00:54:10,960
addendum to the rules for continental empire. What was that one? No hot wars. If you think about Soviet rulers, they were all veterans of World War II through Leonid Brezhnev.

286
00:54:10,960 --> 00:54:19,120
They understood that war is easy to get into, hard to get out of, very unpredictable. For them it was proxy wars. They loved it when the

287
00:54:19,120 --> 00:54:27,120
United States got into hot wars. What is not to like about the Korean War and the Vietnam War with the United States just tied down and Americans tearing each other's eyes out

288
00:54:27,120 --> 00:54:36,880
about these things? Great, great, great. Russians stayed out of it until after Brezhnev's stroke. That's when they make the big boo-boo in Afghanistan.

289
00:54:36,880 --> 00:54:47,360
They go into Afghanistan and then all of a sudden we have loads of fun giving Stinger missiles to the other side, inflicting costs on the Russians. The person who wanted to get into Afghanistan is

290
00:54:47,360 --> 00:55:00,080
Yuri Andropov, who was not a veteran. The uniformed military of Russia said, "Don't go into Afghanistan." Andropov and friends ignored it and did their thing. If you look at Putin,

291
00:55:00,080 --> 00:55:12,560
he has risen to power on a diet of hot wars. He comes to power in the early phase of the Second Chechen War, where he levels the Chechen capital of Grozny and leaves most of the rest of

292
00:55:12,560 --> 00:55:23,360
Chechnya an environmental waste zone. But he sorts that one out. Then he gets quite popular for his war with Georgia in 2008, where he detaches South

293
00:55:23,360 --> 00:55:32,080
Ossetia and Abkhazia from Georgia. Now he wants to build a naval base in Abkhazia because he's having trouble with his base in Crimea.

294
00:55:32,080 --> 00:55:44,720
Then when he eliminates term limits for himself in 2012, which isn't very popular in Russia, he solves that by going into Ukraine in 2014 and walks off with about 7% of Ukrainian territory at

295
00:55:44,720 --> 00:55:55,120
very little cost. And Russians think that's great! If you look at these little stepping stones, this is the way continentalists look at it. You’ve got all these little places lined up.

296
00:55:55,120 --> 00:56:03,920
You want to color it in. That's where Ukraine comes in, in 2022. Putin wants to reverse all these territorial losses.

297
00:56:03,920 --> 00:56:13,440
He wants the green things and the purple things. I don't know if he wants to do the Napoleonic Wars and go all the way to Paris. Who knows? Here's how he looks at

298
00:56:13,440 --> 00:56:22,320
NATO or doesn't look at it. You look at when people join. There are two great big periods of accession to NATO.

299
00:56:22,320 --> 00:56:35,200
One is in the early Cold War with all the smaller countries of Europe, which is everybody. We're all smaller than Russia. The Europeans join to protect themselves from Soviet imperialism.

300
00:56:35,200 --> 00:56:46,080
Then at the end of the Cold War, when these Soviet satellites can finally slip the leash, I've given you all the reasons why, they stampede into NATO. It is not NATO tricking them to join.

301
00:56:46,080 --> 00:56:56,000
They're stampeding in for excellent reasons. They've been proven right. Putin, when he works his magic the second time around in Ukraine in 2022, this is when the

302
00:56:56,000 --> 00:57:05,600
Finns and the Swedes decide neutrality's not going to cut it and they’ve got to join NATO. But when Putin looks at this, he goes, "Oh, they're coming at us in arcs." Well,

303
00:57:05,600 --> 00:57:18,480
this is ludicrous. Russia has posed an existential threat to these places forever. No one today wants to invade Russia. Who'd want it? It's full

304
00:57:18,480 --> 00:57:29,840
of Russians. We want them to stay home. There's no one there who wants Russia. The Russians like to gaslight everybody else, like, "You're the problem." It's been very

305
00:57:29,840 --> 00:57:42,640
effective. Lots of Americans will talk about NATO expansion and say, "Oh, this is why Putin went to Ukraine. You did NATO expansion." He's gaslighting everybody. Russians are the problem. Also it

306
00:57:42,640 --> 00:57:53,280
is their totally dysfunctional domestic system that offers everybody else nothing but problems. Instead of focusing on that and fixing it so they can be a productive member

307
00:57:53,280 --> 00:58:03,120
of the rest of the world, they're not doing that. They're just invading other people. Back to the real problem, what are the prospects of a bromance with these two?

308
00:58:03,120 --> 00:58:19,120
Barring World War III, which would be the great powers going at it… If the great powers go at it, you might well superglue these two. If that happens, whatever side is losing

309
00:58:19,120 --> 00:58:31,040
might well use a nuclear weapon, and then we're going to have toxic plumes everywhere. So we had better get our diplomacy right because there are really serious consequences

310
00:58:31,040 --> 00:58:40,720
for getting it wrong in this particular era. We're at the beginning of a second Cold War with a leadership that has not been chastened by surviving World War I,

311
00:58:40,720 --> 00:58:49,360
the Great Depression, and World War II. Which were the grownups who did a very effective job dealing with the early first Cold War. We haven't got those people anymore.

312
00:58:49,360 --> 00:59:02,800
So if you look at primary adversaries, primary theaters, you don't want them to align for your enemies, because if they do, they'll coordinate. Currently, Vlad the Bad has a Ukraine fixation.

313
00:59:02,800 --> 00:59:13,440
Xi Jinping has got a Taiwan fixation, opposite ends of Eurasia. Xi Jinping is also causing India problems and doing South China Sea island building.

314
00:59:13,440 --> 00:59:27,840
But they're on opposite ends of their two empires. So far, nothing aligns with these two. Even in a world war, their primary theaters probably would not align in the same way that

315
00:59:27,840 --> 00:59:36,320
the Axis primary theaters did not align. They might well disperse their resources in different directions. But the problem is we have

316
00:59:36,320 --> 00:59:48,480
a lot of people with nuclear weapons now. You really don't want to do the hot war. If the West and others manage things correctly, I've given you a huge

317
00:59:48,480 --> 01:00:00,560
historical legacy of fraught relations. Play your cards appropriately and these two will take care of each other. The rest of us can, if we do it right,

318
01:00:00,560 --> 01:00:10,400
not launch trade wars on each other, but rather try to maximize our prosperity so that we get stronger and stronger while they're busy in these wealth-destroying wars.

319
01:00:10,400 --> 01:00:24,080
Putin is just blowing through all of his assets in Ukraine to no purpose. That's how we fought and won the last Cold War. Putin, he's trying to do empire in the age of

320
01:00:24,080 --> 01:00:36,640
nationalism. It's a non-starter. He's dumping all of his ordnance on Ukraine, leaving Siberia wide open. Xi Jinping has moved right in even before the Ukraine war. The Belt and Road Initiative

321
01:00:36,640 --> 01:00:47,200
is peeling away the former Russian sphere of influence in Central Asia. Putin is doing this hot war gambit when Russia's weak and China is strong,

322
01:00:47,200 --> 01:01:03,680
making it particularly damaging. Why would it matter? Siberia has precisely the resources that China now needs and covets and wants to have them contiguous so other people can't mess with them.

323
01:01:03,680 --> 01:01:13,120
In particular they want water because they've blown through their water table in North China. Lake Baikal has over 20% of the world's surface fresh water.

324
01:01:13,120 --> 01:01:24,880
So if you want a quick fix, it's the best one. China is known for big water projects. They've been damming the Mekong and the Yangtze. They've done massive water projects. So

325
01:01:24,880 --> 01:01:40,800
that may be in Russia's future. Who knows? Xi Jinping now holds all of the cards. China has nine times the population, nine times the GNP of Russia, and their per capita GNPs are

326
01:01:40,800 --> 01:01:50,480
converging. Not good news for Putin. The question isn't whether this bromance is going to last forever, but rather when it's going to end. When is Xi Jinping going to decide he's

327
01:01:50,480 --> 01:02:02,240
got the right amount of leverage to get whatever it is he wants? What's going on now illustrates the perils of getting your primary adversary wrong.

328
01:02:02,240 --> 01:02:14,320
Nicholas II fought a recreational war against Japan, the Russo-Japanese War of 1904-1905. He is pouring resources into really an irrelevant theater in those days,

329
01:02:14,320 --> 01:02:25,360
instead of taking those resources and putting it into a railway system that European Russia desperately needed to face off Germany, which it does within the decade and loses.

330
01:02:25,360 --> 01:02:37,600
He is overthrown in part because of bread riots in St. Petersburg, because no foreign aid can get in, because he has inadequate railway system. They'll be built later and it'll be good for Lend-Lease.

331
01:02:37,600 --> 01:02:51,600
So he and his family wind up shot in a Siberian basement. It takes a decade to play out. I don't know how it's going to go for Vladimir Putin on all of this. If we play our cards right,

332
01:02:51,600 --> 01:03:03,280
maybe he'll go down as lonesome. We may have to wait a long time. On the other hand, if we get into a hot war, nuclear war, and in the meantime,

333
01:03:03,280 --> 01:03:08,960
if the United States kills off its alliance system which is one of its greatest strengths, by doing a

334
01:03:08,960 --> 01:03:22,480
gratuitous trade war on our allies, then we have a dust-up with China… Well, let's think about this. Japan tried this go-it-alone, my-way-or-the-highway strategy

335
01:03:22,480 --> 01:03:33,360
with China back in World War II, when China was a failed state, and it wrecked Japan. China now has a population that's a multiple of ours, an industrial base

336
01:03:33,360 --> 01:03:44,960
that's a multiple of ours. Why on earth would you ever want to take them on alone? So I'm going to quote Pericles, who was

337
01:03:44,960 --> 01:04:00,960
the leader of Athens at its height when it was building all these ancient ruins that weren't ruins when they were being built. They're gone. This warning came on the eve of the

338
01:04:00,960 --> 01:04:14,400
succession of Athenian blunders and ancient Athens never recovered: "I am more afraid of our own blunders than of the enemy's devices." Our leaders truly need to ponder this before they

339
01:04:14,400 --> 01:05:42,800
wreck all of us, themselves included. So thank you for your attention. That's what I have to say about Sino-Soviet things.

340
01:05:42,800 --> 01:05:53,840
First question, I want to understand the role of ideology in the Sino-Soviet split. These are the two major communist countries in the world. They have this big ideological, world-changing mission.

341
01:05:53,840 --> 01:06:03,920
We're going to spread communism around the world. That isn't enough to prevent war over, in the case of 1969, literally just some islands in the middle of a river.

342
01:06:03,920 --> 01:06:12,160
There's nothing super consequential at stake here. Why didn't the fact that they were both these communist countries do more to cement the relationship?

343
01:06:12,160 --> 01:06:28,080
I gave you all these bad things that Russia had done to China, which are not small things. Then isn't the problem with communism that the economics of it don't actually work?

344
01:06:28,080 --> 01:06:44,080
Fundamentally these two countries are continental powers, they're continental empires. Sure they change out ideologies. The ideology of the tsars changes out to communism and China is changing

345
01:06:44,080 --> 01:06:54,960
out Confucianism for communism. But the principle that each one should be king of the roost hasn't changed. That would be a mutually exclusive proposition of

346
01:06:54,960 --> 01:07:09,760
who's going to run Asia and then Eurasia, because they have a shared address, this long border. Also with communism, it's supposed to take over the whole world.

347
01:07:09,760 --> 01:07:23,360
We're supposed to have a classless society. That's why we don't even worry about governments and things because they're eventually going to melt away. Well, that's all nonsense. As things

348
01:07:23,360 --> 01:07:34,720
go on further and further, you have dictators more and more entrenched and they're using communism. There's another piece where it's just amazing, about the big lie.

349
01:07:34,720 --> 01:07:39,760
I don't know whether it was Stalin who said it, that if the lie is big enough, people will believe you. It's just so out there,

350
01:07:39,760 --> 01:07:51,760
it's so preposterous, they go with it. Which is this notion that it's the West through these imperial powers, right? And we buy this, that they're not the ones.

351
01:07:51,760 --> 01:08:02,320
But if you look at it, I've given you the data. Then this other one in our own time, this lie about, "Oh, it's the West, it's NATO expansion, this explains Putin."

352
01:08:02,320 --> 01:08:13,920
I’ve given you the data, it's nonsense. Now why the Russians feel obliged to take other people's territory, part of it is feeling good while other people

353
01:08:13,920 --> 01:08:24,000
squirm. There's that aspect. They're not the only people who do those sorts of things. But it's also from being on the plains of Eurasia where, throughout history,

354
01:08:24,000 --> 01:08:33,040
people have invaded you. So you have an established paradigm of how you deal with other people that is deep-seated over thousands of years.

355
01:08:33,040 --> 01:08:41,279
I can't totally answer the question because there are a lot of Russians and a lot of Chinese whose decisions together aggregate to what they do.

356
01:08:41,279 --> 01:08:53,920
I want to understand Stalin's decisions in particular in the case of telling the Communists during the Chinese Civil War that they can't go below the Yangtze River, so that he can

357
01:08:53,920 --> 01:09:03,439
split up the Chinese, the Communists and the Nationalists in China, and leave China weak. We know from all the actions in Stalin's life that he was a devoted communist.

358
01:09:03,439 --> 01:09:12,880
He does collectivization and almost destroys his regime because he's a devoted communist. At the same time, communism says that there has to be this worldwide revolution at some point.

359
01:09:12,880 --> 01:09:21,040
If there is a worldwide revolution, there will be other powerful communist countries. That's implied in the nature of what a worldwide communist revolution is.

360
01:09:21,040 --> 01:09:31,200
But at the same time he doesn't seem communist enough to want China to become fully communist. He cares more about real geopolitik. Tell me about how this works.

361
01:09:31,200 --> 01:09:37,359
His version of communism, the Russians invented it. Actually they didn't, Marx invented it. But they are the guys who think, "Well, we

362
01:09:37,359 --> 01:09:46,800
operationalized it so we should run it forever." So of course Russia is going to be the big communist country. A bunch of Chinese upstarts,

363
01:09:46,800 --> 01:10:00,960
from their point of view, are claiming, "No, no, no, we're going to run the show." But did Stalin as a communist think, "Okay at some point the whole world is going to be communist."

364
01:10:00,960 --> 01:10:14,880
He thought that he personally would be managing the whole world? Unclear. It's a long horizon. In his lifetime he's initially about communism in one country,

365
01:10:14,880 --> 01:10:27,120
then communism on your borders, which is what he's working on. As for the rest of the world, it takes Brezhnev to start going all deep into Africa.

366
01:10:27,120 --> 01:10:36,240
Stalin wasn't interested in, say, India because he thought they're a bunch of lackeys of the British. How do they let themselves be colonized by the British? There's something wrong with

367
01:10:36,240 --> 01:10:48,320
them. You have to also think about where history is at a given moment. He dies in '53, not that long after World War II. How is it all going to turn out?

368
01:10:48,320 --> 01:11:00,000
His relationship with China, as far as he's concerned, is going swimmingly when he's in power. Mao goes there and kowtows in Moscow and does what Stalin wants him to do.

369
01:11:00,000 --> 01:11:09,760
That's Stalin's experience until he, I don't know, chokes on his Cheerios or whatever happened to him that morning. If Stalin hadn't died, do you think a Sino-Soviet

370
01:11:09,760 --> 01:11:20,080
split was still inevitable? Yes, there wouldn't have been Khrushchev's secret speech. Obviously Stalin wouldn't have condemned the cult of personality. But the grievances

371
01:11:20,080 --> 01:11:30,400
you mentioned would still exist. I think the grievances would still exist. Dictators' deaths profoundly weaken their systems because they've got no good succession.

372
01:11:30,400 --> 01:11:41,040
It means you're guaranteed a cat-fight royale going on. That's what one waits for with Putin. Eventually the man will die one way or

373
01:11:41,040 --> 01:11:52,640
another, and there will be a cat-fight royale for what happens after him. Stalin was pretty old when he died and Russians in those days had a lousy diet.

374
01:11:52,640 --> 01:12:06,240
I can't remember if he smoked. His generation mostly did. So I don't know how the future would have run with good old Uncle Joe there for longer. The Korean War would have still gone on longer.

375
01:12:06,240 --> 01:12:22,160
It's really his death, why that war ends then. With the Korean War going on and on, I don't know what the implications are for us or for Mao. This is a really interesting thing to consider

376
01:12:22,160 --> 01:12:29,280
from this lecture. In retrospect, maybe one of the most important things, if not the most important thing that happened in the

377
01:12:29,280 --> 01:12:40,480
20th century, is the Chinese Civil War. At the time, you wouldn't think of this as a major event, especially since World War II had just happened.

378
01:12:40,480 --> 01:12:46,720
If you were living in 1948 or 1949, you wouldn't think of this as the main thing happening in the world. But given that we know in retrospect

379
01:12:46,720 --> 01:12:52,880
this is a really big country with a huge population, we should be paying a lot of attention to its internal political development. I wonder if we should apply the same logic today

380
01:12:52,880 --> 01:12:59,760
where there are other countries with huge populations where we don't think a lot about what's happening. India or Nigeria. I really couldn't tell you the first

381
01:12:59,760 --> 01:13:07,920
thing about Nigeria even though I know that it's a big country with a huge population. Let's not try because I don't know anything about it either.

382
01:13:07,920 --> 01:13:18,880
On China and the Chinese Civil War, there was an understanding that it was a really big deal. That's why Roosevelt—and the British are just thinking he's laughable—keeps

383
01:13:18,880 --> 01:13:36,160
trying to treat China like a great power. The British are saying, "They're not a great power, Franklin, not remotely." But he wants to bring them into the Cairo Conference. He also wants them to be a

384
01:13:36,160 --> 01:13:49,840
veto-wielding member of the United Nations, which by China's military status there's just no way. But Roosevelt is looking, there's no Japan out there, we want to have

385
01:13:49,840 --> 01:13:58,800
something in Asia to counterbalance. But then when you get to the Chinese Civil War, Americans who've fought some big wars, they're looking at it saying,

386
01:13:58,800 --> 01:14:18,080
"It is not feasible for us to alter this outcome." The thinking was — I think it was the Eisenhower archives — that if they lived together long enough, they would go at each other eventually.

387
01:14:18,080 --> 01:14:29,360
I want to zoom in on one particular episode during this period that I think is fascinating. In 1936, the Communists in China kidnapped Chiang Kai-shek, who's the leader of the Nationalists,

388
01:14:29,360 --> 01:14:36,960
and they're about to kill him. Stalin radios in over the Comintern. He says, "No, you can't kill Chiang Kai-shek even though he's your enemy,

389
01:14:36,960 --> 01:14:48,720
he's been massacring you. You gotta let him go." In exchange, Chiang Kai-shek has to promise to create a united front against the Japanese. There are 30,000 communist guerrillas

390
01:14:48,720 --> 01:14:56,400
when they're fighting the Japanese at this period and there are 1.5–2 million nationalist troops. The communists get to claim the prestige of fighting the

391
01:14:56,400 --> 01:15:05,600
Japanese with a fraction of the effort. This just seems like a brilliant move on Stalin's part in retrospect, because if they had killed Chiang Kai-shek, the Nationalist

392
01:15:05,600 --> 01:15:11,040
forces might have dissolved. There would be a puppet government installed by Japan. It would be a terrible thing for Stalin.

393
01:15:11,040 --> 01:15:18,800
It just seems like Stalin makes a lot of right calls in the theater in Asia at this time. What is the understanding, big picture, that lets him?

394
01:15:18,800 --> 01:15:30,000
There are dictators all over the world who stay in power for a long time. Do not dismiss their ability to stay in power. They're surfing quite a wave wherever they live,

395
01:15:30,000 --> 01:15:40,080
of people who butcher each other with great regularity. I would suspect—suspect, because how would I have the evidence—that many of these long-term

396
01:15:40,080 --> 01:15:51,920
dictators have EQs that are off the charts. When you come in the room, they just gut-feeling know whether they're going to cap you or not. If they get a few extra people, so what?

397
01:15:51,920 --> 01:16:03,440
Just so they get rid of all the people who are going to cause them problems. Incredible abilities. Also what Stalin does—I think Saddam Hussein imitated him, but I could be wrong

398
01:16:03,440 --> 01:16:14,560
about that one—is have multiple security agencies with overlapping jurisdictions. No one quite knows who's running the show and who's in power,

399
01:16:14,560 --> 01:16:25,520
but they're all funneling information to Stalin. So he has better information than anybody else. You can use one one day, another another day, which explains how all the heads of

400
01:16:25,520 --> 01:16:37,760
the equivalent of the KGB come and go. Most of the early heads of that wind up getting shot by their successors. You'd think they'd notice a pattern.

401
01:16:37,760 --> 01:16:48,800
They're very good at staying in power. They're really good at managing that problem. It does not bring prosperity. That comes from the world of maritime

402
01:16:48,800 --> 01:16:59,600
trade, of following international law instead of capping your trade partner. You're not going to get a second trade deal if you do that.

403
01:16:59,600 --> 01:17:07,840
It's just so much more wealth-creating, this alternate universe, but that's not where these continentalists are. There's still something

404
01:17:07,840 --> 01:17:20,720
a little under-explained about… Probably lots of things are under-explained. Hitler's another dictator. Forget about having these moves like deciding,

405
01:17:20,720 --> 01:17:27,200
"Don't kill this kidnapped person because it'll be a bad idea in the long run." On the opposite end, he's declaring war against America even when he doesn't have to.

406
01:17:27,200 --> 01:17:38,000
He's just making these crazy moves. Stalin is a special thing to explain. It's not just communism. It's something else that's making him a deft geopolitical actor.

407
01:17:38,000 --> 01:17:49,040
You can do this in retrospect and go, "crazy move," because no one realized that the US productive base could do what it did. Americans didn't even know. You tell me right now how

408
01:17:49,040 --> 01:17:57,120
the future's going to turn out. This is where if you flip it… When you look at history, you think it had to turn out that way because

409
01:17:57,120 --> 01:18:06,160
you can reverse-engineer something, at least a plausible story of why you wind up where you did. It may not even be the right story, but it yields the correct answer.

410
01:18:06,160 --> 01:18:15,520
So you think you got it. But if you look at the future, how's this thing going to end with Xi Jinping and Putin? We don't know. Afterwards, you'll go,

411
01:18:15,520 --> 01:18:32,480
"Oh, something is profoundly stupid based on subsequent events." But we don't know now. There's a Russian proverb which is: Чужа́я душа́ – потёмки (chuzhaya uma potyomki). Someone else's

412
01:18:32,480 --> 01:18:43,920
mind, potyomki, darkness. You ultimately don't know what other people are thinking. You make your guesses, but then they do things. When you showed the maps of China in 1850,

413
01:18:43,920 --> 01:18:54,880
Russia also in 1850, especially 1900, Russia and China are still big countries today. But if you just look at Qing dynasty China, it's overwhelmingly big.

414
01:18:54,880 --> 01:19:04,960
It’s the same with Tsarist Russia before the Brest-Litovsk Treaty. It makes you think that at various points through history, at most points through history,

415
01:19:04,960 --> 01:19:14,640
China was technologically dominant over the West, except during this one crucial period in the 18th and 19th century with the Industrial Revolution in high gear.

416
01:19:14,640 --> 01:19:22,880
Which changes the whole world. Right. It reinforces the idea of how important it is to be at the technological frontier, especially at historically crux-y times.

417
01:19:22,880 --> 01:19:34,000
Of course we're in San Francisco. Many of us here believe that this is especially a crux-y time in the development of technology. Maybe we should continue investing in it instead

418
01:19:34,000 --> 01:19:45,920
of canceling all the science research projects. What will be the long term effects of doing this? You're thinking of America's great strengths, its alliance systems.

419
01:19:45,920 --> 01:19:56,240
Our great strength also used to be being at the head of innovation. We're going to eliminate those things and think it's going to go well for us?

420
01:19:56,240 --> 01:20:07,440
I want to ask about your, I don’t know if you call it a prediction but, your hypothesis that at some point China and Russia will finish each other off or have some sort of conflict

421
01:20:07,440 --> 01:20:12,560
which will be bad for both of them. They won't finish each other off. They're too big to be finished off. Unless we do a nuclear war, then we

422
01:20:12,560 --> 01:20:20,480
might finish all of ourselves off. But the idea that there will be some sort of conflict between these two countries… I agree with the idea that

423
01:20:20,480 --> 01:20:31,360
China has a lot of people and a lot of wealth. Russia has a lot of water, and it has a lot of resources. These are complementary resources. They both have things that the other party has.

424
01:20:31,360 --> 01:20:40,400
The question is, will they attain these things through war or through trade? It seems very improbable to me that… China's got a lot of money.

425
01:20:40,400 --> 01:20:49,280
It can just pay for Russian oil, it can just pay for water. Why would it invade a nuclear-armed nation? I never said it would do that.

426
01:20:49,280 --> 01:21:02,240
It's a question of if Putin feels that his back is up against the wall. What price? We don't really know the terms of their trade. What are the real energy prices that China's

427
01:21:02,240 --> 01:21:16,960
paying for all of this? I don’t know. Maybe some people do know. I suspect that whatever the terms of trade are going to be, the Chinese are going to get a really good deal.

428
01:21:16,960 --> 01:21:32,240
If you look at what happened to Gorbachev at the very end of the Cold War. He's not going to war with anyone, but his internal situation is devolving so rapidly

429
01:21:32,240 --> 01:21:47,040
because their economy collapses. He's already got lousy oil prices. In addition, he has so upset the centralized planning system such that revenues are just

430
01:21:47,040 --> 01:21:57,280
collapsing around him, and he's about to be thrown out of power. He is desperate for the kind of loans that the Germans in particular are willing to

431
01:21:57,280 --> 01:22:10,400
give him in order to get, not only Germany reunited, but West Germany staying in NATO. The next thing is East Germany is going to be in NATO too, and then NATO can have whomever.

432
01:22:10,400 --> 01:22:20,560
Those things are trades he never would have made ever, but he's so desperate. In that case, the West likes to pay people, so instead of paying him a low price,

433
01:22:20,560 --> 01:22:34,960
the West is paying him a high price to back off. In China, I suspect it'll be the opposite. They pay him less and less for whatever it is he wants if he really continues to corner himself in Ukraine.

434
01:22:34,960 --> 01:22:48,720
These terms of trade will get more and more in China's favor. Does that look like the bromance ending? North Korea has for decades been in a very desperate position. That’s quite a bromance.

435
01:22:48,720 --> 01:23:04,160
But there's no risk of North Korea and China going to war. North Korea relies heavily on China. It's not draining China in any way. It's the other way around. That's what Russia's future may be.

436
01:23:04,160 --> 01:23:16,960
Apparently certain Russians, when Putin had invaded the big way in Ukraine, said, "Oh my God, that's our future. It's North Korea. That's where we're headed." That could well be their future.

437
01:23:16,960 --> 01:23:30,240
It'll just keep going as they pour out wealth and the Chinese will try to get very good terms of trade for resources and be niggardly, meaning giving very few things back.

438
01:23:30,240 --> 01:23:44,160
In 1931, when Japan attacks Manchuria, Japan commandeers the Chinese Eastern Railway, which the Treaty of Portsmouth in 1905 said was supposed to be under Russia's purview.

439
01:23:44,160 --> 01:23:53,200
People in the Politburo are telling Stalin, "Look, we’ve got to be aggressive against this." Stalin says, "Look, I don't want to raise tensions against another great power.

440
01:23:53,200 --> 01:24:02,720
Let's just let this slide, let's keep tensions low." This is quite similar to what happened with splitting up Poland in 1939 and then Barbarossa,

441
01:24:02,720 --> 01:24:11,520
where in both theaters he makes this calculation, "I'm going to let certain things slide so that I don't have to face off against these great powers on my border.

442
01:24:11,520 --> 01:24:18,960
I don't want to go to war with them." In the case of Japan, it works because Japan decides to attack China and not Russia. In the case of Germany, it doesn't.

443
01:24:18,960 --> 01:24:33,360
Yeah, in Germany, he's trying to run the same script. He thinks it's going to work for him. That's what the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact is about.

444
01:24:33,360 --> 01:24:46,080
Not remotely. He needed to read Mein Kampf to understand, "No, you're a menu item for Hitler. He's eventually going to come around." In our own day we don't really

445
01:24:46,080 --> 01:25:00,240
want to absorb the bad news. I could be wrong, but I believe that Putin wants not only all of Ukraine, which he says he wants, but also he wants the Baltic states and

446
01:25:00,240 --> 01:25:13,200
he wants to keep on going. This is profoundly bad news. If that's the case, then it means for Europeans at least, they're going to have to divert all kinds of resources to this problem,

447
01:25:13,200 --> 01:25:22,640
which is something they don't want to do. Because like us, they're indebted too, and there are other things they would prefer to spend their money on.

448
01:25:22,640 --> 01:25:35,040
We don't like the bad news that this is where we're at. You have to make nasty trade-offs. Stalin tries to work the same magic and thinks

449
01:25:35,040 --> 01:25:44,320
it's going to work for him and he doesn't get it. He becomes the menu item for Hitler and it just about wrecks him. But it works beautifully against Japan.

450
01:25:44,320 --> 01:25:56,960
It's just interesting to see. It makes sense why he thought it would work. When he's getting information that the Germans are lining up millions of men along this huge border,

451
01:25:56,960 --> 01:26:08,320
he says, "This is British disinformation." He uses the exact same logic when Japan is invading Manchuria, that the idea that he had to fight Japan was British disinformation.

452
01:26:08,320 --> 01:26:16,800
Even though it was a mistake, it helps us understand why he thought this way. There's another concept that's useful: cooperative adversary. I've introduced

453
01:26:16,800 --> 01:26:26,800
this before. It doesn't mean the adversary wants to cooperate with you, but they just don't play their cards particularly well. We're talking about China that has had all

454
01:26:26,800 --> 01:26:37,520
of those rebellions I told you about and civil wars and regional wars. It doesn't have these strong government institutions.

455
01:26:37,520 --> 01:26:47,520
It is much easier to script-write a place like that, to have them do things that are antithetical to their interests. You’ve got a cooperative adversary. The Chinese aren't

456
01:26:47,520 --> 01:26:56,000
trying to cooperate, but from a Russian point of view they might be a cooperative adversary. Whereas with Hitler, Germany has all kinds of institutions and

457
01:26:56,000 --> 01:27:06,480
all kinds of well-educated people. Hoodwinking Hitler is going to be much more difficult than whoever he's dealing with in China in this period.

458
01:27:06,480 --> 01:27:24,400
An interesting connection to make between World War II and this Russo-Chinese relationship is that the way that we used Russia—as this reservoir of military men that we can just ship armaments

459
01:27:24,400 --> 01:27:36,160
to and they can do the dying for us—that’s similar to how Russia uses China in the war against Japan. Well, they're not actually during that war shipping that much to China,

460
01:27:36,160 --> 01:27:48,640
but at least during the Korean War. Korean War, yes. Interestingly, Russia is doing to them the kind of alliance that we had with Russia during World War II. Think about today. It's like the Korean

461
01:27:48,640 --> 01:28:04,640
War in reverse because China's dribbling out aid to Russia. Russia's getting wrecked by Ukraine. Russia's got three times the population or something of Ukraine, but the death rate that

462
01:28:04,640 --> 01:28:17,440
it's suffering is really high. These are only children. This is no longer the 10 children, six children, peasant families in Russia. Look at it from Xi Jinping’s perspective, the

463
01:28:17,440 --> 01:28:26,560
longer this goes on, the better. Just let Putin keep on working his magic and I get to sell him stuff. I'll get him to lower the prices of

464
01:28:26,560 --> 01:28:36,720
resources because the man's desperate. Putin will find himself under a Chinese yoke that he does not like. Then at some point the Russians will

465
01:28:36,720 --> 01:28:48,640
reassess and I don't think they'll like that. The Russians fancy themselves being Europeans and have a whole, I believe, racist package that goes with the Chinese so

466
01:28:48,640 --> 01:28:58,720
that they won't like that whole situation. I don't know how long it takes them to do whatever they're going to do. A lot of things in foreign policy,

467
01:28:58,720 --> 01:29:06,080
you're not going to solve it. They're huge countries, there's no way you're going to solve it. But you need to figure out how to manage it,

468
01:29:06,080 --> 01:29:21,120
to have a blast shield so that whatever fallout comes from their toxic, whatever they're doing, it minimizes how it hits your friends and partners. This is why you should be focusing on maximizing

469
01:29:21,120 --> 01:29:30,240
the economic growth of your friends and partners, because that is the only effective way to deal with them. If you go it alone, who deals with a

470
01:29:30,240 --> 01:29:43,600
bully alone? Always gang up on them. Why would you ever want to go alone against a bully? You'd want to go in with lots of friends. This is what the World War II generation did.

471
01:29:43,600 --> 01:29:55,440
The Marshall Plan, you could never get something through like Congress now. Yet it's passed overwhelmingly in Congress because people get it that you have to spend

472
01:29:55,440 --> 01:30:08,480
real money so the European economies recover. How are they gonna do it? Buying our stuff. It's a tremendous win-win. In strategy, you wanna figure out win-win things instead of these

473
01:30:08,480 --> 01:30:24,640
zero-sum things where, "Oh, I invite you over, I humiliate you, I feel good," and then you're mad forever. It's pointless. Great note to close on. Don't humiliate people. Be kind to each other, yes.

474
01:30:24,640 --> 01:30:36,480
It's a good callback because I've been asking you all these naive questions and you've been very kind to not humiliate me. Thank you all very much for coming.

475
01:30:36,480 --> 01:30:46,960
It is so fun to be able to do these things with a live audience and not trapped inside a dark studio room. I hope we see you guys again on Friday

476
01:30:46,960 --> 01:30:59,120
when we're doing the lecture on the proper way to do war termination. And next Tuesday is about why Russia lost the Cold War. See you on Friday. Thank you, Sarah.
